# The verification gate this project declares for worker steps.
#
# READ BY: internal/scheduler (declaredGate), one reader shared by the integration check, the per-step HEAD
# measurement and the baseline capture — so the project cannot have two different notions of its own gate.
#
# WHY THE KEYS AND NOT A BARE COMMAND. The platform used to infer per-check results by pattern-matching the
# gate's output, which was wrong in principle and broken in practice: raw `node --test` TAP keyed its failure on
# the test NUMBER and dropped it, so failures vanished. The project now DECLARES the format and the platform
# parses that — no guessing, no per-language wrapper.
#
# `format = json` COVERS `go test -json`, which is Go's own machine-readable output (a stream of test events,
# collapsed per package). No gotestsum, no extra tool.
#
# ANYTHING ELSE — no file, an empty file, a bare command — is still legal: a bare command means format
# `exit_code`, which parses nothing and simply records that per-check results were not available. That is an
# advisory note, never a failure.
#
# THE PER-CHECK RESULTS ARE WHAT MAKE THE DIFFERENTIAL POSSIBLE: base vs HEAD, so a step is measured only on what
# IT changed and a repository that is already red can still say something useful about a step.
command = go build ./... && go vet ./... && go test -json ./internal/... ./cmd/...
format  = json
